[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Value Types vs Reference Types & Memory Management in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how .NET manages memory is crucial for writing high-performance applications. At the heart of this memory management model is the fundamental distinction between Value Types and Reference Types."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Differences: Stack vs Heap"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you declare variables in .NET, memory is allocated in one of two primary areas: the Stack or the Managed Heap."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stack: Used for static memory allocation. Variables allocated on the stack have a short lifespan, tied directly to the scope of the method execution. Access is extremely fast."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Managed Heap: Used for dynamic memory allocation. Reference types reside here. The Garbage Collector (GC) manages this memory, cleaning up objects that are no longer reachable."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Value Types"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Value types directly contain their data. Each variable has its own copy of the data, and operations on one variable do not affect another (except when explicitly passed by reference using ref or out)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Examples: All numeric types (int, float, double), bool, char, struct, and enum."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Storage: Typically allocated on the stack (when declared as local variables) or inline within containing reference types on the heap."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"int a = 10;
int b = a; // b gets a copy of the value
b = 20;
Console.WriteLine(a); // Outputs 10"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Reference Types"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reference types store a reference (memory address) to the actual data, rather than the data itself. Two reference type variables can point to the same object on the heap."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Examples: class, interface, delegate, array, and string."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Storage: The variable reference lives on the stack (or heap if part of an object), but the actual object instance is always allocated on the managed heap."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public class Person {
    public string Name { get; set; }
}

Person p1 = new Person();
p1.Name = "Alice";

Person p2 = p1; // p2 points to the exact same heap object
p2.Name = "Bob";

Console.WriteLine(p1.Name); // Outputs Bob"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Boxing and Unboxing"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Boxing is the process of converting a value type to the type object or to any interface type implemented by this value type. Unboxing is the reverse process. This operation has a performance cost because it involves memory allocation on the heap and type casting."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"int number = 42;
Object boxed = number; // Boxing: allocates memory on the heap
int unboxed = (int)boxed; // Unboxing: extracts the value from the heap"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Memory Management & Garbage Collection"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Common Language Runtime (CLR) manages memory allocation and release via the Garbage Collector (GC). The GC uses generations (Gen 0, Gen 1, and Gen 2) to optimize cleanup frequency, keeping high-allocation applications performant by minimizing full memory sweeps."}]
